Maximize utility
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{array}{rrcl}
\displaystyle\min&\frac{1}{2}\delta(\mathbf{w}^{T}\Sigma\mathbf{w})-\mu^{T}\mathbf{w}\\
\textrm{s.t.}&\mathbf{w}^{T}e&=&1\\
\end{array}
\end{aligned}
\]
To incorporate these conditions into one equation, introduce new variables \(\lambda_{i}\) that are the Lagrange multipliers and define a new function \(\mathcal{L}\) as follows:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w},\lambda)&=\frac{1}{2}\delta(\mathbf{w}^{T}\Sigma\mathbf{w})-\mu^{T}\mathbf{w}-\lambda_{1}(\mathbf{w}^{T}e-1)
\end{aligned}
\]
Then, to minimize this function, take derivatives with respect to \(w\) and Lagrange multipliers \(\lambda_{i}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w},\lambda)}{\partial w}&=\delta\Sigma\mathbf{w}-\mu-\lambda_{1}e=0\\
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w},\lambda)}{\partial \lambda_{1}}&=\mathbf{w}^{T}e-1=0
\end{aligned}
\]
Simplify the equations above in matrix form and solve for the weights \(\mathbf{w}\) and Lagrange multiplier \(\lambda_{1}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\delta\Sigma & e \\
e^{T} & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{w} \\
-\lambda_{1}
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu \\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\\
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{w} \\
-\lambda_{1}
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
\delta\Sigma & e \\
e^{T} & 0
\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mu \\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]

